isible by \\(9\\), \\(32 + G + H\\) must be divisible by \\(9\\).\n\nWe need to find pairs \\((G, H)\\) such that \\(32 + G + H\\) is divisible by \\(9\\):\n\n- If \\(G = 0\\), \\(H = 8\\) because \\(32 + 0 + 8 = 40\\), and \\(40\\) is not divisible by \\(9\\).\n- If \\(G = 1\\), \\(H = 9\\) because \\(32 + 1 + 9 = 42\\), and \\(42\\) is divisible by \\(9\\).\n- If \\(G = 2\\), \\(H = 1\\) because \\(32 + 2 + 1 = 35\\), and \\(35\\) is not divisible by \\(9\\).\n- If \\(G = 3\\), \\(H = 2\\) because \\(32 + 3 + 2 = 37\\), and \\(37\\) is not divisible by \\(9\\).\n- If \\(G = 4\\), \\(H = 4\\) because \\(32 + 4 + 4 = 40\\), and \\(40\\) is not divisible by \\(9\\).\n- If \\(G = 5\\), \\(H = 0\\) because \\(32 + 5 + 0 = 37\\), and \\(37\\) is not divisible by \\(9\\).\n- If \\(G = 6\\), \\(H = 10\\) (not valid because \\(H\\) must be a single digit).\n\nSo the only valid pair \\((G, H)\\) is \\((1, 9)\\) and \\((9, 1)\\).\n\n### Step 3: Summing all distinct possible values of \\(G \\times H\\)\n\n\\[\nG \\times H = 1 \\times 9 = 9\n\\]\n\\[\nG \\times H = 9 \\times 1 = 9\n\\]\n\nThus, the distinct possible values of \\(G \\times H\\) that we need to sum are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
